# Line-ending policy
# ====================================================================
#
# Sprint 9 step B — defense in depth for the legal-fingerprint chain.
# The policy engine hashes rule-pack YAML files to produce the
# ``rule_pack_provenance.file_sha256`` field that anchors an audit
# report to the exact regulatory anchor used at scan time. The Python
# code at pqc_audit/policy_engine.py LF-normalizes the bytes before
# hashing, so a wrong client autocrlf setting can no longer
# falsify the verification. This file is the second line of defense:
# it forces eol=lf on the regulatory YAML at checkout/commit so the
# materialized file on disk also matches the canonical form.
#
# Without this, a contributor on Windows could push a YAML edit with
# CRLF endings into the canonical commit, and the digest the engine
# advertises would no longer round-trip the public reference. The
# critic-orchestrator job a0302e7b67e46711 documented the failure
# mode empirically.

# Default: normalise text on commit, leave eol up to OS on checkout.
* text=auto

# Hard-pin LF for files whose byte content is part of the legal
# fingerprint or whose semantics are sensitive to line endings.
pqc_audit/rule_packs/*.yaml text eol=lf
pqc_audit/policies/*.yaml    text eol=lf
tests/fixtures/schemas/*.json text eol=lf

# Vendored reference data (schemas, fixtures) — never normalise.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
